  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "One whale has sung at the wrong pitch since 1989. Fifty-two hertz. Blue whales call between ten and thirty-nine.",
        "visual_query": "north pacific open ocean swell",
        "visual_fallback": "open ocean"
      },
      {
        "narration": "A Woods Hole team first caught it on US Navy listening arrays, hardware built to track submarines and opened to scientists in 1992.",
        "visual_query": "hydrophone research vessel deck ocean",
        "visual_fallback": "research ship"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It has turned up every year since. Aleutians down to California, August through December. One voice, off-key, never answered.",
        "visual_query": "aleutian islands cold coastline",
        "visual_fallback": "cold coast"
      },
      {
        "narration": "So who is it calling? The pitch has crept down to about fifty hertz, which is just what an animal growing older sounds like.",
        "visual_query": "underwater blue whale silhouette",
        "visual_fallback": "underwater light"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Nobody has ever seen it. The leading explanation is a hybrid, part blue whale and part fin whale, with a voice matching neither parent. And in 2010 a sensor off California picked up more than one of them.",
        "visual_query": "pacific ocean surface sunset waves",
        "visual_fallback": "ocean sunset"
      }
    ],
